{
  "decision": "cause_unresolved_no_proven_production_defect_from_available_evidence",
  "execution_scope": "Read-only source and already retained log/receipt comparison. No Rust, product execution, deadline replay, repository changes, assertion changes or budget changes.",
  "findings": [
    {
      "detail": "Original normal-schedule target has one pass and one failure at semantic_source, line 261: expected complete, observed timeout for the recovery request. The published 58-function primary matrix retains 57 pass / 1 fail; no new complete-task acceptance follows from this read-only review.",
      "kind": "established_failure"
    },
    {
      "detail": "The primary and the single serial diagnostic retain identical product SHA d20d2262d9c64553a9d5e796d31430c1e52041d161f776c57cc529c531fee965 and runner SHA e26d785f5904dd01ee26acc34ac1513c3e95c04f331890dc5aed9d15491e62fc. Their differing outcomes do not alone prove scheduler causation or an absence of a race.",
      "kind": "established_identity"
    },
    {
      "detail": "The fixed sequential deadline loop first runs search/headers, then context/headers, search/body and context/body. Primary evidence contains the completed search/headers trace only for that loop. Together with the later semantic_source assertion, this points to the context/headers recovery iteration; its failing response itself was not retained, so this is a control-flow inference rather than a measured response record.",
      "kind": "bounded_scenario_inference"
    },
    {
      "detail": "Session.call stores a returned Value only in its in-memory records. Session.trace writes the complete scenario only after semantic_source and subsequent cache/count assertions. Panic therefore loses the failing recovery Value, its semantic elapsed_us/truncation reason/candidates, HTTP events and end-to-end duration. No such values can be reconstructed from the retained successful scenarios.",
      "kind": "observability_gap"
    },
    {
      "detail": "QueryHandle creates a fresh per-request total control (8000 ms here), then one min(parent_deadline, now+150 ms) semantic child. The same absolute child covers async admission, persisted-scope/health CPU probing, query cache/admission, blocking provider creation, provider gate wait, one HTTP attempt/response processing/cache publication, and exact-recall CPU admission/scan/scope declaration. Healthy loopback response alone does not guarantee completion of all these stages under the child clock.",
      "kind": "deadline_contract",
      "references": [
        "crates/cc-server/src/query_handle.rs:62-113",
        "crates/cc-model/src/query.rs:79-133",
        "crates/cc-search/src/execution.rs:26-42,147-171",
        "crates/cc-server/src/semantic_wiring.rs:831-911,943-1006",
        "crates/cc-server/src/semantic_query_encoding.rs:321-422",
        "crates/cc-server/src/semantic_runtime.rs:524-574,608-651"
      ]
    },
    {
      "detail": "The composition creates a fresh project child CancellationToken for each encoding. Future abandonment uses a separate QueryControl; CPU admission likewise receives an independent control. The async timeout does not itself cancel the shared parent. Timeout cannot be changed into Complete by resetting the child after encoding, and extending 150 ms to the 8 s parent would change the existing contract. No concrete cross-request cancelled-token reuse was found in the reviewed paths.",
      "kind": "reviewed_cancellation_boundary"
    },
    {
      "detail": "semantic_adapter maps every QueryTimedOut from the bounded optional lane to status timeout / semantic_deadline. Provider-gate waiting can also map an acquisition failure into ProviderError::Timeout. The one status assertion does not distinguish scheduler delay, async/CPU queue delay, provider/client setup, wire delay, gate admission, post-response checking or exact scan.",
      "kind": "timeout_is_not_a_unique_stage"
    },
    {
      "detail": "The retained primary search/headers recovery took 6303 microseconds of semantic-lane time, and the isolated context/headers recovery took 10328 microseconds. These are different successful requests and do not bound or diagnose the missing failed request.",
      "kind": "actual_successes_not_failure_measurement"
    }
  ],
  "full_task_accepted": false,
  "next_evidence_if_ci_reproduces": [
    "Preserve the new fixed-source normal-schedule CI result first; keep the original 150 ms semantic child, 8000 ms parent, assertions, and default test parallelism.",
    "To locate a repeat failure, capture its returned recovery Value and semantic lane elapsed/truncation reason before the success assertion, plus HTTP request/headers/body/completion timing and stage/gate admission timings. A separate explicitly identified diagnostic capture must not overwrite historical receipts or count as passing the unchanged primary gate.",
    "Do not choose a production fix solely from the existing serial success. No production patch is justified by this read-only evidence alone."
  ],
